## ===========================================================================
## THE NIGHT JOURNEY TO THE BALL — Book 12 Parts 3-4 (`story.ni:10960-11046`)
## with the Pieter-keyed layers of Book 5B (`story.ni:6578, 6829, 6880-6958,
## 6978, 7032-7035, 7216-7307, 7372, 7424-7428`), Chapter 10.
##
## The change document ("Chapter 10 — The Night Journey to the Ball",
## 2026-09-02) authorizes the chapter from Journey to the Ball beginning on
## Commerce Street to the first step into the keep's Foyer: evening, the
## night market, Lord's Road, the Southern Gate (new), the Pasture, the
## Crossing, the Woods without the sapling, the Clearing and the bolt, the
## tunnel, the wall and the loose brick, the Chapel, the Lower Bailey
## cleaned for the ball, and the Foyer (new). Chapter 4's rooms play again
## in their ball-night state; that layer sits on `night-journey.chord`,
## gated on `bodyguarded`, beside Bobby's-night clauses. Every line is
## Gentry's; Pieter's tree is Chapter 9's stub.
##
## THE RULINGS (change document, Chapter 10):
##   * EXTENT — Commerce Street to the Foyer (David: "A"); the step north
##     into the ballroom is Chapter 11's opening (`ball.chord`), and Chapter
##     XI's row begins on her first visit to it.
##   * PRESSURE — fear without teeth (David: "B"): something of the men or
##     the watch on the night journey, seen or nearly met, no fail state.
##     ONE placeholder, on the first arrival in the Clearing with Pieter —
##     the fountain where they took her the night before; where it lands and
##     what it is are David's (gap 1).
##   * THE RAILS — as the source has them (David: "A"): east from the night
##     market still open; no going back once on Lord's Road; the Woods' wrong
##     trails loop with Pieter grumbling; the Clearing's "You can't turn back
##     now"; up and south from the Lower Bailey refused; south from the
##     Foyer refused. The Southern Gate is the one spur.
##   * WHO SEES HER — nobody new. Pieter's "Jack" slips are habit.
##
## GAPS (change document): the pressure beat's place and lines; the tunnel
## lit and unremarked with nobody carrying a light; no dawdle clock on ball
## night (Chapter 4's was Bobby's); the sapling's way kept from Bobby's
## night — it is a state on the sapling, so moving it offstage keeps it.
##
## NOT CARRIED, with reasons:
##   * Pieter's road mutters "in random order" — Chord has no
##     draw-without-replacement strategy, so the ten play in the source's
##     order, once each (deviation 10 in `secret-letter.story`); the one
##     chance in three per turn is the source's.
##   * The Woods' grumble draws its verb separately (says / grumbles /
##     mutters / pants); the seven lines carry one verb each (deviation 11).
##   * "split" and "sapling" as words for the underbrush while the sapling
##     is gone; "brick" as a word for the wall before the hole is found —
##     conditional vocabulary has no Chord spelling.
##   * "You can't quite fit [the noun] into the hole" — a container's clause
##     cannot name the item; and the hole cannot tell the brick from
##     anything else put into it, so the refusal fires while the brick is
##     already in place and the "slides easily back" line on any insertion
##     while it is out.
##   * `follow trails` with Pieter (the sapling's way or a random one) — the
##     following action is Bobby's; the exits do the same work.
##   * The distant descriptions and the facings (peering is not extended
##     outside the market, as Chapter 4 ruled); the source's themes.
##   * The Journey's tension ratchets (no tension in the port).
## ===========================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE OPENING (`story.ni:10967-10974`), spoken on Commerce Street as the
## preparations become `journey`; the hour turns to night there and the
## purse goes out of play (`commerce-street.chord`).
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define phrase evening-falls
  Evening is falling over the city, and the streets are clearing of people.

  'The ball at Lord's Keep will be starting soon,' mutters Pieter. 'We'd
  better hurry!'
end phrase

## PIETER'S ROAD MUTTERS (`story.ni:10786-10796`): one chance in three a
## turn, anywhere but Commerce Street, ten at most. His block in
## `lords-market.chord` counts them.

define phrase pieter-mutters, cycling
  'Remember, keep that dagger hidden until you absolutely need it,' says
  Pieter. 'It's no good in a sword fight, so your only advantage is
  surprise.'
or
  'I don't know why I let Dame Sandler talk me into this,' Pieter mutters.
or
  'I'm not sure what Dame Sandler expects you to do at this ball,' says
  Pieter. 'Introduce yourself to a lot of people, I guess?'
or
  'When we get there, try to stay in a well-lit, crowded place, okay, Jack?'
  says Pieter. 'Don't go wandering off.'
or
  'Don't worry,' says Pieter, 'I'll have your back. But you're going to have
  to do all the talking.'
or
  'If the Baron tries anything, we are leaving. Immediately,' growls Pieter.
or
  'Try not to get into any fights,' Pieter warns. 'I got you that dagger for
  protection, not so you could wreak your vengeance.'
or
  'Try to do whatever it is you need to do quickly,' says Pieter. 'I don't
  want to stay any longer than we have to.'
or
  'If we manage to survive tonight, it'll be a miracle,' mutters Pieter.
or
  'I have a bad feeling about this,' says Pieter under his breath.
end phrase

## `story.ni:6578` — the night market's pole with Pieter.

define phrase not-the-time-for-climbing
  Now is not the time for climbing around!
end phrase

## `story.ni:10962`

define phrase pieter-cant-go-back
  'We can't go back now,' says Pieter. 'The ball will be starting any minute
  — and Dame Sandler will have my head on a platter if I don't get you to it
  on time!'
end phrase

